{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel journeyed from Be'erot benei Ya'akan to Moserah; there Aaron died.</b> Rashi explained: but did he not die at Hor ha'Har, etc. There are several difficulties in this matter. The first is: if the intention is to inform us that they went back from Hor ha'Har to Moserah, it should have said that they journeyed from Hor ha'Har to Kadesh, etc., as far as Moserah — why does he omit all the stations and mention only that they journeyed from Be'erot benei Ya'akan to Moserah? The second: why is it said here ״בְּאֵרוֹת בְּנֵי יַעֲקָן״, whereas in parashat Masei he did not use the word Be'erot? The third: here he infers that they went backward eight stations, whereas in parashat Chukkat Rashi explained that they went back seven stations. The fourth: ״שָׁם מֵת אַהֲרֹן״ — Rashi explained that they held a eulogy there; why specifically there in Moserah and not beforehand? The fifth: what is the logical derivation for saying ״וַיְכַהֵן אֶלְעָזָר בְּנוֹ תַּחְתָּיו״, for this is not part of the matter of reproof? The sixth: ״מִשָּׁם נָסְעוּ הַגֻּדְגֹּדָה וּמִן הַגֻּדְגֹּדָה יָטְבָתָה אֶרֶץ נַחֲלֵי מָיִם״ — why did he detail this encampment at Yotvatah more than all the other encampments, and why did he mention here ״נַחֲלֵי מָיִם״ and did not mention this in parashat Masei? The seventh: what is said, ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא הִבְדִּיל אֶת שֵׁבֶט הַלֵּוִי״ — this separation took place at the beginning of the forty years, and what is its relevance here? The eighth: what he said, ״וּלְבָרֵךְ בִּשְׁמוֹ עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ — to which day is he pointing with his finger to say that this is the one about which he said ״עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ — as if from here onward they were no longer separated? The ninth: what Rashi explained, that the Levites brought them back — whence does Rashi have to say so? If it is a received tradition, we accept it. The tenth: what Rashi explained, לְלַמֶּדְךָ שֶׁקָּשָׁה מִיתַת הַצַּדִּיקִים כִּשְׁבִירַת הַלּוּחוֹת — but the entire parashah speaks of the second tablets, and what is said ״אֲשֶׁר שִׁבַּרְתָּ״ — the death of Aaron is not joined to this conclusion. The eleventh: what Rashi explained, that this day on which they sought to break away from him was as difficult as the day they made the calf — the calf, who mentions its name? For it is not mentioned in the entire parashah." "And now incline your ear and hear, and behold I shall explain all this by means of a small suggestion which we have already recalled in parashat Chukkat (22), that at the death of Miriam the water was lacking, for every righteous person resembles a surging spring, as is stated in Avot (6:1), and whoever does not eulogize him as fitting does not feel the loss of a source of living waters; therefore it is just that his waters should run dry. And so it happened to them at the death of Miriam, as it says (Numbers 20:1): ״וַתָּמָת שָׁם וַתִּקָּבֵר שָׁם״ — for there her burial was immediate, and she was at once forgotten as dead from the heart, and it is not said of her ״וַיִּבְכּוּ אוֹתָהּ״; this is proof that they did not eulogize her, therefore the waters were lacking. So too now, at the passing of Aaron, they feared the lack of water — that what happened at Miriam's passing might happen to them. Moreover, there loomed over them the terror of King Arad, and out of fear they were compelled to turn back; and during this retreat there was also the fear of water upon them, lest the well be lacking from them. Therefore they made an encampment at every place where they found water. Although on the forward journey they did not encamp at the water-places, because the journey was short, nevertheless on the retreat they encamped there, for the retreat was after the death of Aaron." "Now between Moserot and benei Ya'akan there was one place called Be'erot, because there were many wells there, and it was not far from benei Ya'akan — so much so that the people of benei Ya'akan drank from those wells, and therefore the place was called ״בְּאֵרוֹת בְּנֵי יַעֲקָן״, meaning the wells of benei Ya'akan. And this is proof that Be'erot was close to benei Ya'akan, and the journey was too short to make an encampment there. Therefore when they traveled forward and not backward, they went in a single stretch from Moserah to benei Ya'akan and did not encamp at Be'erot, for the journey was too short to encamp there, and at that time there was no fear of water before their eyes. But when they turned back after the death of Aaron out of fear of King Arad, they also encamped at Be'erot, for they feared that perhaps the well would suddenly cease, as it ceased at Miriam's death. Therefore he counted eight stations on the retreat, but on the forward journey there were only seven. And therefore he counted on the backward retreat only ״וַיִּסְעוּ מִבְּאֵרוֹת בְּנֵי יַעֲקָן מוֹסֵרָה״, for he comes only to inform us of this novelty, that they encamped at Be'erot — which they had not done on the forward journey. And when they arrived at Moserah, a place that had no water at all for their needs, and they feared the lack of the well, they went and eulogized Aaron as though he had died there, for they said that in the merit of the eulogy they would be delivered from the lack of the well. And in this they sinned, for they had not taken it to heart to eulogize him beforehand, because they relied on the place of Be'erot and Yotvatah, a land of streams of water, that lay before them. And the verse says: ״וַיְכַהֵן אֶלְעָזָר בְּנוֹ תַּחְתָּיו״ — that they had no cause to fear the lack of water on account of the passing of the righteous one, for whoever raises up a son who is righteous, it is as though he did not die (Bava Batra 116a), and even though Aaron died, nevertheless the merit of his son stands to deliver them from the lack of water. And from the overall thrust of his reproofes — that they still stood in their rebellion and were lacking in faith, and that even after this they encamped at Yotvatah because it was a land of streams of water, implying that without this they would not have encamped there — one gathers the whole." "And what he said, that the Levites brought them back, etc. — his proof is from what is said, ״בָּעֵת הַהִוא הִבְדִּיל אֶת שֵׁבֶט הַלֵּוִי״ — this was certainly said concerning the time of the making of the calf. And what is meant by ״עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״? Rather, this is its explanation: although in the matter of the calf the Levites were chosen and their merit is preserved for all generations, nevertheless there was no need for that merit except ״עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ and no more — for on this day they did a good deed, such that even had they not been chosen for the service of YHWH from of old, it would have been fitting for them to be chosen from this day forward. And that is the meaning of that the Levites brought them back from that crooked path upon which they had been walking. And from this we infer, that he compared the reward of the Levites for what they did now to what they did at the time of the calf — hear from it that this day, on which they took the lead to break away from him, was as difficult as the day on which they made the calf." "And likewise what he explained, that the death of the righteous is as difficult as the breaking of the tablets, is clarified according to this manner: for since it says ״וַיְכַהֵן אֶלְעָזָר בְּנוֹ תַּחְתָּיו״ and this is joined to this parashah that speaks of the second tablets — hear from it that Elazar his son arose in his stead just as the second tablets arose in place of the first ones that were broken, and from this it follows that the passing of Aaron resembles the breaking of the tablets, for knowledge of opposites is one. And he comes to accuse them in his reproofs, for turning back out of fear of King Arad, when they should have trusted that the merit of Aaron would do battle for them even in his death — just as we find that the Ark containing the broken fragments of the tablets would go out and come with them to battle, so too the departed righteous one, who resembles the broken fragments of the tablets — his merit would stand for them even in his death. For so Moses was not answered until he said: ״זְכֹר לַעֲבָדֶיךָ לְאַבְרָהָם״ etc. And according to this suggestion, this entire passage is reproof over transgression, and the whole matter is linked from beginning to end."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "The final sentence, וּמִכְּלַל תּוֹכְחוֹתָיו... אֶחָד, is rendered hyper-literally; the Hebrew tail of comment 2 as given appears truncated and its full syntactic resolution is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:15.908334127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}